        "findings": [
          "Both public owners create the same DevelopmentOperation with the caller-supplied original until, invoke one private execution boundary, then consume the operation exactly once through finish_development after any normal Result.",
          "Under cfg(test), observe_operation still places the action inside test_observation::with_operation closure. All ? and explicit early returns resolve inside that closure, the private method returns its Result, and the public owner reaches finish_development.",
          "Under cfg(not(test)), observe_operation expands to its action block. All ? and early returns now resolve at the private method boundary instead of escaping the public owner, so the same final observation is retained without the immediately invoked outer closure that Clippy rejected.",
          "The development Option is a local variable independent of the mutable witness owner. Each helper borrows it only for the synchronous call; phase guards are scoped inside that call. Returned Vec/unit and owned Error do not borrow it, so the borrow is finished before the public method moves the Option into finish_development.",
          "Each complete operation body was independently extracted from Git base and current source and compared after exactly four leading spaces were restored on each nonempty line. Both are byte-identical after that reindent.",
          "Original 250ms MAX_EXCHANGE, identical until values, read/write deadlines, precommit deadline, commit_uncertain sequencing, authenticated reply construction, committed CAS late-reply treatment, and current-operation final deadline remain unchanged.",
          "No definite source-level blocker found. This review is not a Rust compilation, Clippy pass, native execution result or performance claim."
        ],
